  "estimatedMinutes": 1,
  "statement": "Given \\(x\\gt0,\\) \\(y\\gt0,\\) \\(x\\gt y,\\) and \\(z\\ne0.\\) The inequality which is not always correct is:",
  "answerChoices": [
    {
      "label": "A",
      "text": "\\(x+z\\gt y+z\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "B",
      "text": "\\(x-z\\gt y-z\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "C",
      "text": "\\(xz\\gt yz\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "D",
      "text": "\\(\\dfrac{x}{z^2}\\gt\\dfrac{y}{z^2}\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "E",
      "text": "\\(xz^2\\gt yz^2\\)"
    }
  ],
  "answer": {
    "letter": "c",
    "value": null
  },
  "solutions": [
    {
      "id": "sol.ahsme-1951-p21.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "Because \\(z\\) may be negative, multiplying \\(x\\gt y\\) by \\(z\\) reverses the inequality and gives \\(xz\\lt yz.\\) The operations in the other choices preserve the inequality because \\(z^2\\gt0.\\)\n\nThus, the correct answer is C.",
      "hints": [
        "Adding the same number preserves order, but multiplying does not always do so",
        "Test the statements when \\(z\\lt0\\)"
      ],
      "images": []
    }
  ],
